  - id: conductor-call-is-uncancellable
    class: inject
    when: { write: "*.rs", contains-any: ["call_zome", "CallZomeOptions"] }
    dedupe-of: "elohim/elohim-storage/src/services/head_batch_resolver.rs (module doc: the fallback rule + the in-wasm BATCH_EXTERN_BUDGET discipline) + .claude/skills/p2p-design-gate/SKILL.md (the conductor call you cannot cancel)"
    why: >
      `HcClient::call_zome` has no timeout and no cancellation path (`hc_client.rs:405`) — a
      caller-side timeout abandons the call while the conductor keeps executing it with nobody
      listening, still holding the read permit whose saturation the timeout was reacting to. The
      T13 spike (2026-08-08-conductor-call-deadline-capability-spike.md) qualifies this: the
      client API already declares a timeout (`CallZomeOptions::timeout`) but enforces it
      CLIENT-SIDE ONLY, and most slow-call cost is DB-permit queue-wait that IS cancellable-on-drop
      (a tokio semaphore permit returns) — only the wasm BODY is non-interruptible once started.
      Design every conductor-touching surface so the WORK is bounded BEFORE the call is made:
      batch externs (`content_store::resolve_content_heads_local` /
      `resolve_canonical_elections`) carry their OWN in-wasm deadline
      (`BATCH_BUDGET_DEFAULT_MS`/`BATCH_BUDGET_CEILING_MS`) and return partial results
      (`unattempted`, never a failure); callers size batches
      (`services::head_batch_resolver::HeadBatchResolver`) and interpret `unattempted`/
      `stop_reason`, never bolt a caller-side timeout onto `call_zome` and call it bounded. If you
      are adding a new `call_zome`/`CallZomeOptions` call site, ask: is the WORK bounded on the
      WASM side before this call is made, or does a caller timeout merely abandon a conductor
      that keeps running?
