# elohim/elohim-storage/src — Rust storage implementation

HTTP handlers, EPR/blob resolution, P2P dataplane, views, and service modules for the
elohim-storage Rust crate. Editing conventions are in `elohim/elohim-storage/CLAUDE.md`.

## EPR/blob resolution invariant (gate-enforced)

**Peers are the store; doorways are projections with peer-fallback.** The EPR/blob
resolution path (`http.rs` app-resolution handlers, `sharding.rs`, `shard_service.rs`,
`epr_head.rs`, `epr_service.rs`, `epr_atom_service.rs`) MUST never return a hard
"App not found" 404 for a known-EPR-head / missing-blob case without a peer-fallback
branch or an EPR-head-aware syncing-status response.

**The shortcut lives at `http.rs:5589` and `:5601`.** The live failure it caused
(alpha-B, 2026-06-29): the `blobHash` pointer was null while the blob bytes were
present locally — the resolver hard-failed instead of healing from a peer.

The `peer-fallback-invariant` inject rule fires as an advisory when any `.rs` write in this
directory contains "App not found" or "App ZIP not available". It catches reintroduction of the
shortcut pattern at edit time without blocking legitimate edits. It names **no validator**: the
rule stands on its class ladder and its `dedupe-of` pointer to the invariant's canonical home
(the backlog record). A `validator:` key naming a detector the resolver does not implement is the
canon's own C7 violation — advertise ≠ serve — so the phantom key was dropped (2026-08-02, plan
task P4.1) while every word of the rule's teaching survived. If a semantic peer-fallback detector
is ever written, it arrives as a registered `epr:validator-*` and this rule graduates then.

**The fix landed** (T17): `get_blob_or_heal` (`http.rs`, `BlobHealOutcome`) now runs the
shared local-read + peer-heal sequence behind BOTH `GET /blob/{hash}` and the `/apps/`
resolver, finalizing via `blob_fetch::finalize_fetch_success` (atomic inventory+event
pair). The invariant now guards REGRESSION: new NotFound arms must route through that
shared sequence rather than re-introducing a bare 404. (Historical shortcut lived at
http.rs:5589/5601, alpha-B 2026-06-29; pointer propagation is the CRDT plane, landed
2026-07-02.)

See: `genesis/data/timeline/backlog/dataplane-peer-fallback-and-blob-replication.md`

## Heal invariant — fills-never-moves (gate-surfaced)

**A self-healing loop with a weaker information source than the state it overwrites converts every
restart into a rollback.** The projection-reconcile heal (`src/p2p/projection_reconcile.rs`) and the
stamp path (`src/db/content_diesel.rs` — `stamp_declared_head`, `stamp_declared_head_mode`,
`StampMode`) must obey: heal FILLS absence, it never MOVES authority. `StampMode::Declare` is only
for a **canonical** answer — the zome now returns `canonical: bool` (a coordinator-only, hot-swapped
change); a fallback resolve (`canonical == false`, e.g. a cold conductor falling through to the
root-author election while the canonical link is not yet retrievable) MUST stamp `GapFill`, which may
fill an empty row but never displaces a declared/adopted row.

The live failure (defect class #2, adam-alpha, 2026-07-11): two minutes after the first-ever
cross-conductor adoption, the restarted node's heal restamped the OLD head over the adopted canonical
row — for 2,838 divergence-queued rows, serially, after every restart. The blind first fix (defect
class #3) keyed the guard on *who* writes and froze forward adoption; the cure was making the SOURCE
name the authority it carries (`canonical`), not gating on the writer.

The `heal-fills-never-moves` rule is a **binding of concern class C2 (monotonic authority)** —
`policy: c2-monotonic-authority@2` in `.claude/epr-meta/policies.yaml`. Define-once-bind-many: the
registry row owns the guarantee, its cross-family statement (the same class bit the zome
link-selector's arrival ordering and the epr-cli/REA sidecar's append-order "latest") and its dated
incidents; this manifest owns only the *placement* — the `when:` scope and the `StampMode`
vocabulary the guarantee wears at this seam. It carries **no `validator:` key**: a binding may not
redeclare the policy's predicates.

**The pin moved to @2 on 2026-08-02 (plan task P4.1), and the move was a decision, not a default.**
@1's predicate was `dedupe-of` — a bind-don't-redefine advisory that fired on *every* write
touching the stamp vocabulary. @2 carries the registered detector
`epr:validator-heal-fills-never-moves`, which fires on the shape instead: (a) a **net-new**
`StampMode::Declare` call site outside the canonical-channel list — `src/db/content_diesel.rs` (the
deliberate-declare wrapper, the guard arm, their in-file contract tests) and
`src/services/head_adoption.rs` (the own-conductor act after `adopt_peer` mints the link); or (b)
added lines touching the guard (`canonical_move_verdict`, the `HealCanonical`/`GapFill` arms,
`moving_declared_row`, `SkippedStale`, `stamp_declared_head_mode`) while the file cites **none** of
the contract tests `elohim/elohim-storage/seam-registry.yaml` registers for it — the blind-fix
shape, where those tests were the only thing that told the two guards apart. Arm (b) stays silent
when the registry registers no test for the file: an uncited decision point is an honest gap the
**census** owns, not a finding this validator may mint. Still `inject`: a false positive costs one
advisory line, never a blocked write. A binding pinned to @1 keeps @1's semantics until it
re-declares — that is what makes the version a declared dependency rather than recency.

See: `genesis/docs/content/elohim-protocol/history/2026-07-12-substrate-convergence-five-defect-arc.md`
and the concern canon: `.claude/epr-meta/concerns.yaml` (C0, C1, C3–C5, C6b, C7–C14) +
`policies.yaml` (C2, C6a).

## Bounded work (C6a — gate-surfaced, 2026-08-02)

The `bounded-work` rule binds `c6a-bounded-work@2`: **every loop, sweep and retry ladder carries a
declared budget it provably respects — and a retry policy against an uncancellable call IS a loop
even when no `loop` token appears in the diff** (`74fbdf2d7`, the instance that named the clause).
The class graduated out of `concerns.yaml` into the enforcement registry when
`epr:validator-bounded-work` was registered; the concerns.yaml row is superseded, not deleted.

The detector is **precision-biased on purpose**: it flags an *added* line carrying an unambiguous
shape (a bare `loop {`; `max_retries`/`max_attempts`/`retry_count`/`backoff`/`RetryPolicy`/
`.retry(`) inside a crate that registers decision points, when no budget vocabulary appears within
±12 lines. It will miss real instances — a sweep written as an iterator chain, a ladder assembled
from a config struct three files away, and above all the responder-below-requester **inversion**
(`1b445b481`), which is an ordering fact no textual scan can see. That is the correct trade for an
advisory: a fuzzy gate that denies teaches agents to route around governance. Declaring the budget
in a `bounded-work: <budget>` comment satisfies it — the guarantee is a *declared* budget, so the
declaration IS the fix, and it is what a reviewer and the runbook's mint-then-quiet probe can then
check. Local vocabulary: paced ingest is `drain_publish_queue` + wait-for-drain (solved twice
already); a third pacing scheme here is a diagnosis failure, not a design.

Calibrated, not asserted (2026-08-02): replayed against this crate at its **worst case** — every
one of the 494 `src/**/*.rs` files treated as if written fresh, so every line counts as added —
the detector fires on **24**. A real edit adds a handful of lines, so the live rate is far below
that; the number is here so the next person can re-measure it instead of trusting this sentence.
The dominant false-positive source was doc comments naming `max_retries` while *describing* a
budget; comment lines are now skipped, which is what took the worst case from 34 to 24. Re-run the
calibration before widening the shape set — a shape that doubles the noise buys nothing an
advisory can spend.

## The conductor call you cannot cancel (gate-surfaced, 2026-08-08)

`HcClient::call_zome` has no timeout and no cancellation path — a caller-side timeout abandons
the call while the conductor keeps executing it with nobody listening, still holding the read
permit whose saturation the timeout was reacting to (the failure compounds exactly when it
hurts most). The T13 spike qualifies this without changing the tactic: the client API already
declares a timeout (`CallZomeOptions::timeout`) but enforces it CLIENT-SIDE ONLY, and most
slow-call cost is DB-permit queue-wait that IS cancellable-on-drop (a tokio semaphore permit
returns) — only the in-wasm BODY is non-interruptible once it starts. The deadline is a missing
MESSAGE to the conductor, not a missing concept.

The head-plane trust-gradient program's answer (L1, `content_store::resolve_content_heads_local`
/ `resolve_canonical_elections`): design every conductor-touching surface so the WORK is bounded
BEFORE the call is made. Batch externs carry their OWN in-wasm deadline
(`BATCH_BUDGET_DEFAULT_MS`/`BATCH_BUDGET_CEILING_MS`, `batch_deadline_admission` in the
content_store zome's own seam-registry.yaml) and return partial results (`unattempted`, never a
failure); callers (`services::head_batch_resolver::HeadBatchResolver`) size batches and interpret
`unattempted`/`stop_reason`, never bolt a caller-side timeout onto `call_zome` and call it
bounded.

The `conductor-call-is-uncancellable` rule fires as an advisory on any `.rs` write in this
directory touching `call_zome`/`CallZomeOptions` — the reminder at the exact point a new
conductor-touching call site is written. It names no validator (the same honesty discipline as
`peer-fallback-invariant` above): the shape "is the work bounded before this call, or does a
caller timeout just abandon a conductor that keeps running" is a judgment call, not a
mechanizable predicate.

See: `.claude/skills/p2p-design-gate/SKILL.md` ("The conductor call you cannot cancel") and
`genesis/docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-08-conductor-call-deadline-capability-spike.md`.

## Governance note

This manifest records three gate-surfaced invariants (peer-fallback on the resolution path;
fills-never-moves on the heal path; bounded work on the sweep/retry paths) plus the guide-star. No
general authoring gate is warranted for the rest of the directory (operator WIP is active; other
editing conventions live in `CLAUDE.md`). Each inject advisory is the lightest signal that surfaces
its lesson where the drift recurs — and none of them names a validator the resolver does not
implement, which is the honesty half of the same discipline.

## Guide-star (operator-set, 2026-07-24)

The `dataplane-guide-star` inject fires on edits touching the resilience/distribution
plumbing's load-bearing symbols. It carries the telos every plumbing sprint serves:
**requisite variety with the hyperscaler — k8s-grade powers over a p2p dataplane — so
that anyone, grandma first, has a real place to exit their data to community.** The
signals are a sensemaking substrate (many folds, many witnesses), the convergence bar
is same-footprint-through-any-doorway, and the destination is earned forgetting: trust
solid enough that people stop performing the surveillance vigilance the enclosed
platforms trained into them. Advisory class (`inject`) — it teaches at the point of
edit, never blocks. It names no validator: the guide-star is a *telos*, not a
mechanizable predicate, and the rule stands honestly on its class ladder and its
`dedupe-of` pointer back to this section.
